  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Thursday: Dry, warm weather continues with high\npressure lingering while the Gulf of AK system approaches \nlater. Skies will be mostly clear through tonight. Clouds \nincrease from the west and north Thursday as some middle to high\nlevel moisture starts in overrun the warm sector of the \napproaching systems. Lows tonight will be in the 40s to near 50,\nwith some upper 30s in the sheltered northeast valleys. Highs \nThursday will be about 10 degrees above normal in the 70s.\n\nFriday to Sunday: A dynamic cold front makes its way across the\nareas Friday, with a colder upper trough taking up residence \ninto the weekend. Rain chances come to the Cascades, with the \nhighest risk near the crest, Friday morning; chances expand to \nthe northeast mountains late Friday morning to afternoon, then \nto the rest of the eastern third of WA and ID late Friday \nafternoon to Friday night. Saturday the upper low continues \nprecipitation chances in the Cascades and northern mountains and\nID and expands them out across central WA too. The chances wane\ninto Sunday. Snow levels start between 7-8kft Friday, then fall\nto 5.5-7.5kft by Saturday evening, then 4.5-5.5kft Saturday \nmorning and afternoon. Overall rain amounts look light away from\nthe mountains and even then the highest precipitation amounts \nare near the Cascade crest. In those mountains, some light snow \naccumulation is in the forecast: generally 0.5 to 2 inches, but \nlocally higher amounts are possible. These is mainly the higher \nmountains. Hikers and campers at these elevations and higher \nshould be prepared for light snow and freezing temperatures.\n\nWinds will be the other notable feature of this system. The \nincoming front and colder air and deepening low toward the front\nrange of the Rockies will lead to a tightening gradients. \nSpeeds increase in the mid to later afternoon. Peak winds are \nforecast Friday between 1 pm to 8 pm, out of the west-southwest \nat 20-30 mph with gusts of 30 to 40 mph. This will be especially\nfrom the lower lee of the Cascades across the basin toward the \nPalouse/Spokane/CdA area. Possible impacts are blowing dust \nacross the Columbia Basin where soils remain dry. Gusts will \nalso create difficult travel conditions, especially for high \nprofile vehicles, and could blow around unsecured objects. \nIsolated power outages are also possible.\n\nWinds turn more northerly Saturday into Sunday. Saturday \nbreezy/gusty winds continue across the Okanogan Valley, \nWaterville Plateau, and Columbia Basin. Sunday wit breezy \nconditions will be broader again, with gusts of 30 to 35 mph and\nthe another chance for blowing dust.\n\nTemperatures drop through this weekend, with highs in the \nmid-60s to mid-70s Friday falling to the mainly the 50s Saturday\nand Sunday. Lows will be in the 40s Saturday morning, then 20s \nand 30s Sunday and Monday morning. So frost and freezing\ntemperatures are in the forecast.  \n\nMonday to Wednesday: The new week starts dry and continued cool,\nthen another wave moves in later Tuesday into Wednesday with\nshower chances across the mountains and eastern third of WA and\nID. Precipitation amounts look light. Highs will be in the 50s\nto low 60s, with lows in the upper 20s to 30s. /Solveig",
